## Matrix Concept

One row of the software layer matrix as a card: identity (`layer`, `layerName`, `lane`, `concept`, `symbol`), the crawl-owned evidence columns (`implemented`, `evidenceTier`, `auditStatus`, `whereImplemented`, `reference`, `catalogMatch`, `provenance`, `domainKit`), and the human-owned tracking columns (`owner`, `workState`, `notes`).

```ts
import { MatrixConcept } from '../matrix-concept';
@field concept = linksTo(MatrixConcept);
```

**Two owners, one card.** Columns A–M mirror the crawl and are overwritten wholesale on refresh; N–P (`owner`, `workState`, `notes`) are edited in this realm and preserved across refreshes — the seed script's `--refresh` mode keeps them. That split is why `workState: Done` can honestly coexist with `implemented: false`: the human said done, the crawl has not counted it yet, and the tracker surfaces exactly that disagreement instead of hiding it.

Evidence tiers are ranked: Platform and Catalog shared count as implemented; Catalog listing and POC realm are evidence on the way there; no tier means no evidence. The fitted tile carries the tier as a colored edge so a wall of tiles reads as a coverage heat map.

The linked examples span the range: a Platform kernel concept, a built-by-hand row the crawl has not counted, and a domain-kit concept at POC tier.

**Non-goals:** holding rollups (counts live in the tracker's queries), linking to the implementing modules (where-implemented is a crawl-written string, not a link, because most targets live outside any realm), and enforcing the crawl/human column split at the field level — the seed script owns that contract.
